    "text_blocks": [
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "Recognizing when your home needs a full roof replacement\u2014rather than a localized repair\u2014is critical for surviving Utah\u2019s heavy winter snow loads, intense summer UV rays, and high mountain winds.",
        "reference_indexes": [
          0,
          1,
          2
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Key Signs Your Utah Roof Needs Replacement"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Roof Age (20+ Years): Standard 3-tab asphalt shingle roofs in Utah typically last 15 to 20 years, while architectural/dimensional shingles (like GAF Timberline HDZ Go to product viewer dialog for this item.Go to product viewer dialog for this item. ) last 25 to 30 years. If your roof is past the 20-year mark, wear is usually systemic rather than isolated.",
            "snippet_links": [
              {
                "link": "https://www.google.com/search?ibp=oshop&prds=pvt:hg,pvo:48,mid:576462892632231031,imageDocid:11024746716892727326,gpcid:7738169664190394463,headlineOfferDocid:4279335607372905182,catalogid:10580140300060570873,productDocid:17186695621331965102,rds:PC_7738169664190394463%7CPROD_PC_7738169664190394463&q=product&sa=X&ved=2ahUKEwiMnf3Xt8uXAxWKQTABHacUPbcQxa4PegoIAggACAAIERAE"
              },
              {
                "text": "GAF Timberline HDZ Go to product viewer dialog for this item.",
                "link": "https://www.google.com/search?ibp=oshop&prds=pvt:hg,pvo:48,mid:576462892632231031,imageDocid:976025478759605817,gpcid:7738169664190394463,headlineOfferDocid:4279335607372905182,catalogid:10580140300060570873,productDocid:17186695621331965102,rds:PC_7738169664190394463%7CPROD_PC_7738169664190394463&q=product&sa=X&ved=2ahUKEwiMnf3Xt8uXAxWKQTABHacUPbcQxa4PegoIAggACAAIERAF"
              }
            ],
            "reference_indexes": [
              1,
              2
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Curling, Buckling, or Cracking Shingles: When shingle edges cup upward, claws downward, or show deep cracks and missing tabs, the material has lost its flexibility and can no longer seal against wind-driven rain and heavy snow melt.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              2,
              3
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Heavy Granule Loss in Gutters: Finding piles of dark, sand-like grit in your gutters or downspout splash zones means your shingles' protective UV armor has worn away, accelerating heat and weather deterioration.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              2,
              4
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Sagging or Dipping Roof Decking: Standing across the street and viewing the ridgeline should reveal a straight line. Any visible dip, wave, or sag means the plywood or OSB decking underneath has absorbed moisture and lost structural integrity\u2014a safety emergency that requires immediate attention.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              2,
              3
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Multiple or Recurring Leaks: A single leak around a pipe boot or flashing can be patched. However, water stains on ceilings in multiple rooms, recurring attic dampness, or daylight visible through roof boards indicate the underlying underlayment and system have failed.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              2,
              3
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Interior Warning Signs: Musty attic odors (indicating mold), peeling paint on upper floors, rusted nail tips from condensation/leaks, or unexplained spikes in winter/summer energy bills point to failing roof ventilation and weatherproofing.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              2,
              5
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Utah-Specific Considerations"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Material Lifespans in Utah Climate:",
            "list": [
              {
                "snippet": "3-Tab Asphalt Shingles: 15\u201320 years"
              },
              {
                "snippet": "Architectural Shingles: 25\u201330 years"
              },
              {
                "snippet": "Synthetic & Cedar Shake: 30\u201350 years (requires fire treatment and regular maintenance)"
              },
              {
                "snippet": "Metal Roofing (Standing Seam / Exposed Fastener): 40\u201370 years (handles heavy snow exceptionally well)",
                "reference_indexes": [
                  1
                ]
              }
            ],
            "reference_indexes": [
              1
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Permitting & Contracts: In Utah, local communities generally require contractors to pull a permit before tearing off and replacing a full roof (though permits are typically not required if simply laying a new shingle layer over an existing one). Always ensure your contractor provides a detailed written contract outlining itemized costs, change orders, and start dates, and verify they carry proper \"Open Roof\" insurance coverage to protect against sudden mountain storms.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              6,
              7
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "If you'd like, share:"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "The approximate age of your current roof"
          },
          {
            "snippet": "The type of material you have now (shingle, metal, tile)"
          },
          {
            "snippet": "What specific damage or leaks you're noticing"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "I can help you figure out whether you're looking at a minor fix or a full replacement."
      }
    ],
